  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Vibrissea truncorum, commonly known as the water club mushroom, is a small aquatic fungus in the Vibrisseaceae family characterized by its club-shaped fruiting bodies. This species is adapted to freshwater environments and is notable for its unique ecological role in aquatic ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Club-shaped fruiting bodies",
      "description": "The mushroom features distinctive club or clavate-shaped ascomata that are typically small and gelatinous in appearance",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Habitat association",
      "description": "Found on submerged wood and plant debris in fast-flowing streams and clean water environments",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Gelatinous texture",
      "description": "The fruiting bodies have a wet, gel-like consistency that helps distinguish them from terrestrial mushroom species",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Vibrissea truncorum thrives in cool, clean flowing streams and brooks throughout New England, typically growing on submerged woody debris and aquatic plant material.",
